I ran out of tools before measuring anything, so H43 is still untested. The B200 Modal function was stopped for the whole session. Every `probe_b200` call failed (3 health checks plus the gate probe), and so did `compile_b200`, all with `ConflictError: function … is stopped`. Only `get_kernel` and `predict_score` worked. That means no STEP 1 timing, no compile, no STEP 2/3 and no run_tests. I didn't call run_tests because it runs on the same stopped GPU function.

The candidate below therefore ships the unchanged r0 anchor on every row, so it has nothing to offer the merge. I also included the complete STEP 1 gate kernel in a third file, `i_gate_conv.cu`, wrapped in `#if 0` so it builds to an empty unit. It has never been compiled or run, and the next session can probe it straight away. One bug in it is already fixed: the shared-memory size for the 32,128 R=4 variant. A desk-check found no other bugs.

### Rationale
STEP 1 is fully designed, but none of it is measured. The gate kernel has 288 threads: 8 conv warps and 1 producer warp that issues per-row 1-D bulk copies. It runs `16·(W/PW) = 256` lanes, with one 2×7 (64,96) or R×4 (32,128) output patch of one channel per lane. Rows are padded to `W+8` with zero pad columns, and the channel stride is ≡16 mod 32 words so the two channels in a warp use disjoint banks. It writes the fp16 d tile into the SW128 B layout and keeps the LN partial sums in registers. A desk check gives an smem problem at 64,96 that STEP 2 must solve: x ring 2×62.5 KB + d tile 48 KB + statistics 14 KB = 188 KB. That leaves no room for a double-buffered d tile or for the W1′ ring.

